  "summary": "The post-release degradation is a hard failure of the CheckoutService/PlaceOrder path: checkout's error_ratio went from 0 to 1.0 in the after window and every observed order from the new checkout instance after ~05:41:57 failed at PaymentService/Charge, with the failing response text being payment's own application error 'Payment request failed. Invalid token. app.loyalty.level=gold' (checkout sees rpc status 13 / Charge client status 2). Before the release checkout had zero error spans for an hour (PlaceOrder and Charge client spans all non-error), payment's Charge server spans had zero errors, and payment made no flagd ResolveFloat calls. Kubernetes data show the release is configuration-only for checkout: pod checkout-7bcc99987c-9cx98 (uid 56416307-4df5-49b5-b836-e64e4a898fa7) was replaced by checkout-7765446c4b-qvd72 (uid e90ba4a1-ff20-44d7-98ec-5610a5828fca) with an identical checkout image_id (sha256:5ab0d7b9003206a32c72ef48b42a535c3f70565251d7d832f86fe9f249f16635), while the payment pod (payment-6f7bc865d6-bfxbv, uid 6998b25a-8cd8-48d1-bcc9-6edc1ff46661, image sha256:33f327b3...) kept one unchanged identity across the whole window. The old instance's Charge calls were all non-error and stopped at handover; the new instance's Charge calls were still non-error in the 2-minute windows ending 05:36:57, 05:37:57, 05:39:57 and 05:40:57 (e.g. a successful Charge at 05:39:21) and then all failing from the window ending 05:43:57 onward, so the pod's configuration alone does not make every charge fail from startup - the rejecting condition is attached to the requests. The deepest evidence-backed cause is therefore in the request the new checkout instance sends: payment's unchanged charge handler (charge.js:37) rejects it as an invalid token and reports the request attribute app.loyalty.level=gold. A second candidate that is not excluded is the flagd flag 'paymentFailure': payment began evaluating it (ResolveFloat, flagd impressions variants 'off' and '100%', reason STATIC) exactly in 05:38:57-05:42:57, but only one of the four sampled failing charges contains a flag evaluation, most evaluations in that burst returned 'off', and failures continued after the last '100%' increment, so the flag is not shown to be the cause. flagd, cart, currency, product-catalog, quote and shipping showed no new error signals, and frontend/frontend-proxy errors are the propagated user-facing 500s."
 },
